[Black "Example 208- Lasker- Em. vs. Rubinstein- A.- St Petersburg 1914"]
[Result "1-0"]
[FEN "5r2/8/6p1/1pkp4/5PP1/3K4/1P6/5R2 w - - 0 1"]

 { The white rook is ideally placed behind the passed f-pawn, so Lasker starts pushing it. } 1. { -KEY- } f5 $1 gxf5 2. gxf5 Rf6 3. Rf4 $1 { Seizing the fourth rank. } b4 4. b3 { Black is now short of solid moves. Note that this is a consequence of his rook being awkwardly positioned in front of the passed pawn - he would very much have liked the two rooks to change places. } Rf7 ( { This lets the passed pawn advance one step further; but } 4... Kb5 5. Kd4 Kc6 6. Ke5 { was equally bad for Black. } ) 
5. f6 Kd6 6. Kd4 Ke6 7. Rf2 $1 { Preparing a decisive switch of angle. } Kd6 ( { After } 7... Rxf6 8. Rxf6 Kxf6 9. Kxd5 { , followed by Kc4xb4-a5, White obviously wins. } ) 
8. Ra2 $1 Rc7 ( { Or } 8... Rxf6 9. Ra6 { , transposing to the previous note. } ) 
9. Ra6+ Kd7 10. Rb6 { In this desperate position, Black resigned. } 
 1-0

