 { White's plan is rather obvious: advance his queenside pawns with the help of his king, while the rook should be sacrificed for the g-pawn at the right moment. } 1... { -KEY- } Rf2+ $2 ( { This check just helps White to improve his king. A better chance in practice was } 1... Rg5 $1 { placing the rook behind the passed pawn, when White would have to find } 2. a4 $1 ( { the natural } 1... Rf2+ 1. Rg3 $2 { allows } Kg6 3. Kd3 Kf5 4. a4 Ke5 $1 { when Black manages to use the king in the battle against the connected pawns, with a theoretical draw following } 5. Ke3 Rg8 6. b5 Kd5 7. Kf4 Ra8 8. Ra3 Kc5 9. Kxg4 Kb4 10. Ra1 Ka5 11. Kf5 Re8 { - Hecht, now that these have been confidently blocked } ) 
g3 3. Rd1 g2 4. Rg1 Kg6 5. a5 Kf6 6. a6 Ke5 7. a7 Rg8 8. Kb3 $1 Kd5 ( 8... Ra8 9. Rxg2 Rxa7 10. Rd2 { is similar } ) 
9. Rxg2 Ra8 10. Rc2 $1 ( { the materialistic } 9... Rf2+ 9. Rg7 $2 { only draws after } Kc6 { with some kind of fortress } ) 
Rxa7 11. b5 Kd6 12. Kb4 $1 Rc7 13. Rd2 $1 { followed by b5-b6 and Kb5. } ) 
2. Kb3 Kg6 3. a4 Kf5 4. a5 Ke4 ( { After } 4... Rf3 5. Kc4 Rxd3 6. Kxd3 Kf4 7. a6 { , only White queens. } ) 
 ( 4... Kf4 { is refuted by } 5. a6 g3 6. Rxg3 $1 Kxg3 7. b5 $1 { (the impatient } 7. a7 $2 { runs into } Rf3 $1 { , when White can't stop the rook from reaching the a-file) etc, as observed by nayoncohr. } ) 
5. Rd8 $1 { Unlike his opponent four moves ago, Berg pays attention to the idea of placing the rook behind the passed pawn. } ( { In contrast, } 5. Rc3 $2 Rf3 $1 { would eventually lead to a queen and pawn vs. queen ending. } ) 
Rf3+ 6. Ka4 Rf1 7. Rg8 Rg1 ( { Or } 7... Kf3 8. a6 g3 9. Kb5 g2 10. a7 Ra1 11. Kb6 Kf2 12. b5 g1=Q $11 13. Rxg1 Kxg1 14. Kb7 { and White wins. } ) 
8. Kb5 { Unable to parry the looming advance of the white pawns, Black resigned. } 
 1-0

