 ( { This example is extracted from my book 'Play the Sicilian Kan'. If it was White to move, then } 1. Rc7 { , followed by Kg3 and Ra7, placing the rook in its optimum place behind the passed pawn, would probably suffice for a draw, despite the two pawn deficit. However, in the game it was Black's turn, and by a simple rook manoeuvre it was he who managed to put a rook on a7 . } ) 
 { -KEY- } Rf4+ $1 2. Kg3 ( { On } 2. Ke3 { , Black can proceed as in the game: } Rf5 3. Rc7 Rf7 4. Rc5 Ra7 5. Ra5 Kf7 6. Kd4 Ke6 { Now if } 7. Kc5 { preventing the black king's walk to b6, then } Rd7 $1 { , starting actions on the opposite flank; e.g. } 8. Rxa6 Kf5 9. Ra5 Kf4 10. Kc6 Rd1 { , followed by Rh1-h5, winning the g5-pawn. } ) 
Rf5 { Gaining a tempo. } ( { The immediate } 2... Rf7 { should work too; e.g. } 3. Ra5 Ra7 4. Kf4 Kf7 5. Ke5 Ke7 { with similar play to the previous note. } ) 
3. Rc7+ Rf7 4. Rc5 Ra7 { Black has completed the rook manoeuvre and should now activate his king. } 5. Ra5 Kf7 { Heading for b6. } ( { Of course } 5... h6 $2 { would be completely counter-productive: } 6. gxh6 Kxh6 7. Kh4 Kg7 8. Kg4 Kf6 9. Kf4 { and White holds. As usually occurs, pawn exchanges favour the defending side. } ) 
6. Kf4 Ke6 7. Ke4 Kd6 8. Kd4 Kc6 9. Kc4 Kb6 10. Re5 Rf7 ( { Avoiding the trap } 10... a5 $2 11. Re6 Kb7 12. Re7 Kb8 13. Re8 { with a draw. } ) 
11. Kb3 ( { Or } 11. Re6 Ka5 12. Re5 Ka4 { followed by }  Rc7 ) 
Rf3+ 12. Kb4 ( { After } 12. Kc4 Rf4 13. Kb3 Rf5 { , Black wins in similar fashion. } ) 
a5+ 13. Ka4 Rf4+ 14. Kb3 Rf5 { Here, Black needs the pawn exchange in order to create a second passed pawn. } 15. Re7 Rxg5 16. Rxh7 Kc5 { The king is now directed to the more useful g-pawn. } 17. Rd7 { The white rook prevents that idea, but it can't stay on the d-file for long. } Rg4 18. Kc3 ( { Or } 18. Ra7 a4 19. Ka3 Kd5 20. Ra5 Ke4 $1 21. Rxa4 Kf3 { with an easy win. } ) 
Rg3+ 19. Kc2 a4 20. Kb2 g5 21. Rd8 g4 22. Rd7 Rf3 23. Rg7 a3+ 24. Ka2 g3 25. Rd7 Kc4 26. Rd8 Rd3 27. Rg8 Kc3 28. Rg7 Kd2 
 0-1

