 { Material is equal, but strategically Black is much better as he possesses an outside passed pawn (without rooks on the board, this factor would indeed be decisive) and, just as important, his rook is ideally placed behind it. } 1. { -KEY- } d5+ $2 { White follows the principle 'Passed pawns should advance!', but in this case the pawn will just become exposed on d5. Furhoff outlines a better plan: 1) fight for the kingside space with h2-h4; 2) take the e4-square from Black's king by f2-f3; 3) try to assign the blocking task on the a-file to the king, so that the rook can be used actively. } ( { A possible variation: } 1. h4 $1 f6 2. f3 { (apart from controlling the e4-square, this also enables a future g2-g4 in order to exchange pawns; besides, it makes sense to place the kingside pawns on different ranks, so that their future liquidation by the black rook takes more time) } f5 3. Kc3 Kb5 ( { or } 3. d5+ Kd5 4. Kd3 f4 5. Kc3 { continuing the waiting game } ) 
4. Rc4 a4 5. Rc5 Kb6 6. Kb2 $1 a3 7. Ka2 { (now that the white rook has been liberated, it is ready to create disorder in the enemy camp) } Rd8 8. Rc4 Kb5 9. Rc5 Kb4 10. Rc6 Rxd4 11. Rb6 $1 ( { a clever intermediate check, avoiding } 11. Rxg6 $6 Rd2 { and the white king becomes cut off from the second rank; } ) 
Kc3 12. Rxg6 Rxh4 13. Kxa3 { with chances for a draw - Furhoff. } ) 
Kd6 2. Kd4 h4 $1 { A useful move that grabs some space and makes the h-pawn more powerful in a potential passed pawn race. } 3. f4 f6 4. h3 Ra7 $1 { Such a zugzwang method is typical when the stronger side has managed to place his rook behind the passed pawn. } 5. Ke4 ( { Obviously, any rook move, such as } 5. Ra1 { , runs into } a4 ) 
Kc5 6. f5 g5 $1 { Avoiding pawn exchanges and limiting the enemy king. Now again White finds himself short of a solid move. } 7. Rd4 Re7+ ( 7... Kd6 { at once is also thinkable, although as observed by Anunnaki, after } 8. Ra4 Re7 9. Kd4 { , Black can't play } Re5 $2 { in analog fashion to the 11.Ra4 subline, due to } 10. Rxa5 { . That's why it makes sense to first deflect White's king from the d-pawn, using an intermediate check on e7. } ) 
8. Kd3 Rd7 9. Ke4 Re7+ 10. Kd3 Kd6 $1 { The d-pawn is neutralized once and for all. } 11. Rc4 ( { Or } 11. Ra4 Re5 12. Rxa5 Rxd5 $1 13. Rxd5 Kxd5 { and Black wins thanks to his favourable pawn structure; e.g. } 14. Ke3 Ke5 15. Kf2 Kxf5 16. Kf3 Ke5 17. Ke3 f5 18. Kf3 Kd4 19. Kf2 Ke4 20. Ke2 f4 21. Kf2 f3 $1 22. gxf3 Kf4 23. Kg2 Ke3 { - Furhoff. } ) 
Kxd5 12. Rd4+ Kc5 13. Rc4+ Kb5 14. Rc8 Re5 { Seeing that he'll lose a second pawn, White resigned. } 
 0-1

