 ( { Now let's see some examples where it is the defender's rook that manages to place itself behind the passed pawn. This is indeed a fundamental defensive method in the endgame, whose power we recently witnessed in Caruana-Anand.With White to move, } 0... Kd6 0. Ra3 { would be very strong, but Smyslov will never let that happen. } ) 
 { -KEY- } Rc1+ $1 2. Ke2 Ra1 { The rook goes behind the passed pawn. } 3. Rd4 ( { Or } 3. Rd8 Kf7 4. Ra8 Kg6 { with similar play as in the game, but with the white rook less efficiently located in front of the pawn. Generally speaking, if the rook can't be placed behind the pawn, then the side angle is the lesser evil. } ) 
Ra3 $1 { Cutting the enemy king off from the third rank, while eyeing the weak g3-pawn. If we compare this endgame toExample 184 (Andersson-Christiansen), here White's chances are reduced by two factors: 1) the a4-pawn is more remote to the white king than Andersson's b5-pawn; 2) Lipnitsky's pawn structure is more vulnerable to an invasion by the black king. } 4. Kd2 Kf7 { There is no hurry to take the g3-pawn. } 5. Kc2 ( { Incidentally, had White prevented Black's next move by } 5. h5 { then after } Ke6 6. Kc2 Rxg3 7. Kb2 g5 $1 { Black achieves counterplay anyway - Smyslov. } ) 
Kg6 $1 { Active defence; the king will be instrumental in Black's kingside play. } 6. Kb2 Rxg3 7. a5 Kh5 $1 { Thus Black doesn't just attack the h-pawn, he also prepares the defensive manoeuvre ...Rg6-a6. } 8. a6 Rg6 9. a7 ( { Or } 9. Ra4 Rb6 10. Kc3 Rb8 11. a7 Ra8 12. Kd4 Kxh4 13. Ke5 Kg4 14. Ra1 g6 { and White can't really make progress without jeopardizing the full point - Smyslov. } ) 
Ra6 10. Rd7 g6 $1 ( { Black avoids getting his king cut off after } 10... Kxh4 11. Rxg7 ) 
11. Rxh7+ Kg4 12. Kb3 Ra1 $1 ( { The impatient } 12... Kxf4 $2 { runs into } 13. Rc7 { , intending } Kg3 14. Rc3 $1 { , getting the rook to the a-file by means of an intermediate check no matter where Black's king goes. } ) 
13. Kb4 Kxf4 14. Kb5 ( 14. Rc7 { can now be met by } Kg4 { since White's king on b4 obstructs the Rc4-a4 maneuver, which played a key role in the previous note. } ) 
Kg3 { Black has managed to organize sufficient counterplay, and the rest is easy. } 15. Kb6 f4 16. h5 gxh5 17. Rxh5 f3 18. Rg5+ Kh2 19. Ra5 Rxa5 20. Kxa5 f2 21. a8=Q f1=Q 
 1/2-1/2

