 { The previous examples all had in common that one side possessed a passed pawn on the queenside. Such rook endings are indeed quite frequent in practice, and they have been extensively analysed within endgame theory. Here is one more ending of this type. } 1... { -KEY- } Rd2 { Black hurries to tie the enemy rook to the pawn, and prepares ...Ra2 whenever White moves the a-pawn. } 2. Kg2 ( { The space-gaining } 2. g4 $5 { was interesting as well, preventing Black from an ideal kingside structure with pawns on h5, g6 and f7. } ) 
h5 3. a4 ( { a) Obviously, White does not benefit from the pawn trade after } 3. Rxh5 Rxa2 ) 
 ( { b) But } 3. Kf3 { was also interesting, getting the king a bit closer to the a-pawn. } ) 
Ra2 4. h4 g6 5. Ra8+ Kg7 6. a5 { White's main plan is the transfer of his king to the opposite flank, but first he advances the a-pawn to a6, since time will be a big issue once Black initiates his kingside counterplay. } Ra3 7. a6 Kf6 8. Kf1 Ra2 9. Kg1 Kf5 { In similar style toExample 213 (Lipnitsky-Smyslov), Black brings his king as close as possible to the white kingside pawns. } 10. Kg2 Ra3 11. Ra7 $1 Kf6 $1 ( { Gurevich's last move was an astute try in practice, in the sense that if Black continued his kingside offensive with } 11... f6 $2 { then White could change plans decisively with } 12. Ra8 Kg4 13. a7 $1 { - Gurevich. a) In similar spirit to the previous example, the black king is now far away from the 'safety zone' on g7-h7, and any active attempt would expose it to a rook check; e.g. } g5 $2 14. hxg5 fxg5 15. f3 ( { b) passive defence is equally insufficient, e.g. } 13... Kf5 14. f3 Ra2 15. Kf1 { and the white king walks all the way to h6, after which Rb8-b5+ and Kxg6 decides, which actually occurred in some games, such as W. Unzicker-E. Lundin, Amsterdam Olympiad 1954. } ) 
 ( { c) Or } 13... Ra2 14. Rf8 Rxa7 15. Rxf6 { with mate. } ) 
 ) 
12. Kf1 Ra2 13. Ke1 Kg7 $6 { Having seen the rest of the game, this seems like the loss of a vital tempo. } ( { After } 13... Ke6 { it is not clear how White should continue; e.g. } 14. Kd1 $6 Rxf2 15. Rc7 Ra2 16. a7 f6 17. Kc1 Kf5 18. Kb1 Ra6 { with much better chances of a draw than in the game. } ) 
14. Kd1 $1 { Evidently, White can't make progress without sacrificing a kingside pawn. } Kf6 ( { After } 14... Rxf2 15. Rc7 Ra2 16. a7 Kf6 17. Kc1 Kf5 ( { Gurevich suggests } 17... Ke6 18. Kb1 Ra5 19. Kc2 f5 20. Kb3 Kd6 { as a better option, although after } 21. Kb4 Ra1 22. Rg7 { Black is still a long way from the draw. } ) 
18. Kb1 { It transpires that Black has no adequate square for his rook; e.g. } Ra4 ( { or } 18... Ra5 $2 19. Rc5 $1 ) 
 ( { while } 18... Ra6 19. Rxf7 Kg4 $2 { is met by } 20. Rg7 $1 { intending } Kxg3 21. Rxg6 { - Gurevich } ) 
19. Rxf7 Kg4 20. Rf4 $1 ) 
15. Kc1 Rxf2 16. Kb1 $5 ( 16. Rc7 Ra2 17. a7 { was possible, transposing to the previous note, but Gurevich goes for more. } ) 
Rf5 ( { Gurevich gave } 16... Rf3 $6 17. Kb2 Rxg3 18. Rxf7 Kxf7 19. a7 { as winning for White, although after } Rg2 20. Kb3 Rg3 21. Kb4 Rg4 22. Kb5 Rxh4 23. a8=Q $11 Rf4 { , it is actually a draw according to Nalimov's tablebases. Nevertheless, I suspect that Black's task remains difficult in practice. } ) 
17. Rc7 { White finally switches to a better angle for his rook. } Rb5+ $2 { This just helps the white king to approach the a-pawn. } ( { Correct was } 17... Ra5 18. a7 Ke6 { transposing to 14...Rxf2 above; } ( { but not } 18... Kf5 $2 19. Rc5 $1 { - Gurevich. Such a deflecting check is the reason why the c-file is better than the b-file for the white rook here, so that Black's recapture doesn't come with check. } ) 
 ) 
18. Kc2 Ra5 19. a7 Kf5 20. Kb3 $1 { Preparing a bridge on a4, which would force Black to sacrifice his rook for the a-pawn much earlier than desired; i.e. when the white king is still rather close to the kingside. } ( { In contrast, after the obvious } 20. Rxf7 $6 Kg4 { followed by ...Kxg3, Black's drawing chances are improved. } ) 
Kg4 ( { Or } 20... Ra1 21. Rxf7 Kg4 22. Rf4 $1 Kxg3 23. Ra4 { and wins - Gurevich. } ) 
21. Rc4+ Kxg3 22. Ra4 Rxa7 23. Rxa7 Kxh4 24. Kc3 $1 ( { King first; after } 24. Rxf7 $2 Kg3 { the h-pawn saves Black. } ) 
Kg3 25. Kd2 g5 ( { Or } 25... h4 26. Ke1 Kg2 27. Ra2 Kg1 28. Ra4 { etc - Gurevich. } ) 
26. Rxf7 h4 27. Ke2 g4 28. Kf1 Kh2 29. Rf2+ Kh1 30. Rf4 h3 31. Kf2 h2 32. Re4 
 1-0

