 { As mentioned in the introduction to the current section, exceptions to the 'place your rook behind the passed pawn' principle exist. Here is one. } 1. { -KEY- } Rg6 $1 { Preparing a side attack, the correct angle in this particular rook ending, so as to prevent the black king from hiding on a2. } ( { The natural } 1. Ra6 $2 { would lose after } Kd3 2. Ra8 Kc3 3. Kf2 a3 4. Ra7 ( { or } 4. Ke2 a2 5. Kf2 Rh1 { etc. } ) 
Kb3 5. Rb7 Ka2 6. Ke2 Rb1 7. Ra7 Kb2 8. Rb7 Ka1 9. Ra7 a2 { followed by 10...Kb2 - a position we already came across inExample 192 (Zhukova-Pogonina), with reversed colours and flanks. } ) 
 ( { However, the similar } 1. Rh6 $5 { would also work, e.g. } Ke3 2. Rh4 { with a later rook transfer to the f-file, in similar fashion to the main line. } ) 
Ke3 ( { In contrast, the black rook can't improve its angle; e.g. } 1... Rb1 $2 2. Rg4 ) 
2. Rg4 $1 { Tying the enemy rook to the pawn. } ( { Placing the rook on the third rank is a bad idea: } 2. Rg3 $6 Kd2 3. Rf3 $2 ( { White could still draw by } 3. Rg4 a3 4. Rg3 Kc2 5. Rf3 a2 6. Rf2 { etc. } ) 
Re1 { followed by 4...Re4, Kc2-b2 etc. } ) 
a3 3. Rg3+ Ke4 ( { If the black king approaches by } 3... Kd2 { then } 4. Rf3 Kc2 5. Kh2 Kb2 6. Rf2 { again prevents the liberation of the enemy rook. } ) 
4. Rb3 Ra2+ ( { a) } 4... a2 5. Ra3 ) 
 ( { b) or } 4... Kd4 5. Rf3 { are draws. } ) 
5. Kg3 Kd4 6. Rf3 Ra1 7. Kg2 $1 { Preventing the check on g1. Unable to make progress, Black agreed to a draw. } 
 *

