 { In this final example, the white rook did really impressive work.Again White has a material advantage, but Black's connected passed pawns must be dealt with correctly. } 1. { -KEY- } Ra7+ $1 { Heading for c7, where the rook will control the enemy passed pawns, while participating in the battle for the opposite flank as well. } Kf8 ( { The desirable } 1... Kf6 $2 { failed to } 2. Rd7 ) 
2. Rc7 c3 3. Ke2 h6 4. Kd3 d4 5. g4 { Having neutralized the enemy pawns, White is now ready to create a passed pawn of his own on the kingside. } Bf6 ( 5... g5 $1 6. f5 Bf6 { was more tenacious - Botvinnik, who also suggests the improvement 2.g4 (or 3.g4), followed by g4-g5, thus eliminating this defensive possibility. } ) 
6. h4 $1 { Gaining space while facilitating the creation of a passed pawn. } Kg8 ( { After } 6... Bxh4 $2 7. Kxd4 Bf6 8. Kd5 $1 { , the entrance of White's king decides. } ) 
7. Ke4 Bg7 8. g5 hxg5 9. hxg5 Kf8 10. Kd3 ( { Since } 10. f5 $6 gxf5 11. Kxf5 $2 { would run into } d3 { , Botvinnik instead prepares a zugzwang. } ) 
Kg8 11. Rc6 Kf7 12. Ke4 $1 { Unfortunately for Black, he is now short of a solid move. } Bh8 13. Rc7+ Kg8 14. Rc8+ Kh7 15. f5 $1 { This advance works here, due to the awkward position of Black's king and bishop. } gxf5+ ( { Or } 15... d3 16. Kxd3 gxf5 17. g6 $1 Kg7 18. Kxc3 { and White wins comfortably thanks to the badly placed bishop on h8 - Botvinnik. } ) 
16. Kxf5 Bg7 ( { Again } 16... d3 { is of no avail: } 17. g6 Kh6 ( { or } 17... Kg7 18. Rxc3 ) 
18. Rxh8 Kg7 19. Rh7 Kg8 20. Rc7 d2 21. Kf6 { with mate. } ) 
17. Re8 $1 { A clever switch of angle to create a mating threat on the h-file. } d3 ( { a) Or } 17... c2 18. g6 Kh6 19. Re2 $1 { with similar play. } ) 
 ( { b) } 17... Bh8 { is equally insufficient due to } 18. g6 Kg7 19. Rc8 $1 { and the pawns fall by themselves. } ) 
18. g6+ Kh6 19. Re3 $1 Bd4 ( { After } 19... Be5 20. Rxe5 Kg7 { (there was the threat of Re7-h7) } 21. Re7 Kg8 22. Kf6 { , White gives mate. } ) 
20. Rxd3 c2 21. Rh3+ $1 { The rook again heads for c7 in order to control the passed pawn from behind. } Kg7 22. Rh7+ ( { Black resigned. Let's see a possible conclusion: } 22. Rh7 Kg8 23. Rc7 Bb2 24. Rxc2 Bd4 25. Rc7 Bb2 26. g7 $1 Kh7 ( 26. Rh7+ Bxg7 27. Kg6 { comes to the same thing; } ) 
27. Rf7 $1 Bc3 ( { or } 27. Rh7+ Bxg7 28. Kg5 { with similar play; } ) 
28. g8=Q Kxg8 29. Kg6 Bd4 30. Rd7 Bc5 31. Rd8 Bf8 32. Re8 { But note that if all the pieces are moved one file to the left (i.e. Ke5 , Rb7 and Pf6 vs. Kf8 and Ba2 ) it is a theoretical draw, a fact that we will come back to in the last example of this book. } ) 

